<article class="env-corollary" id="01HW">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01HW">Corollary <span data-tag="01HW">4.6.2.9</span>.</a> Let $f: X \rightarrow X'$ and $g: Y \rightarrow Y'$ be categorical equivalences of simplicial sets. Then the induced map $(f \star g): X \star Y \rightarrow X' \star Y'$ is also a categorical equivalence of simplicial sets. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      We have a commutative diagram of simplicial sets 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ X \diamond Y \ar [r]^-{f \diamond g} \ar [d]^{ c_{X,Y} } &amp;  X' \diamond Y' \ar [d]^{ c_{X',Y'} } \\ X \star Y \ar [r]^-{ f \star g} &amp;  X' \star Y',} \]
</div>
<p>
       where $f \diamond g$, $c_{X,Y}$, and $c_{X',Y'}$ are categorical equivalences (Remark <a data-tag="01HU" href="/tag/01HU">4.6.2.7</a> and Theorem <a data-tag="01HV" href="/tag/01HV">4.6.2.8</a>). Invoking the two-out-of-three property (Remark <a data-tag="01ED" href="/tag/01ED">4.5.4.5</a>), we conclude that $f \star g$ is also a categorical equivalence. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></article>
